From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SacredTrees <121399723+SacredTrees@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 21:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] amigaos: -mcas does atomics with CAS inline on a 68020 to
 68060

bebbo's m68k.cc leaves FL_CAS out of every AmigaOS CPU ("no CAS on the
AMIGA"): the chipset does not take CAS's locked read-modify-write cycle in
Chip RAM.  So even -m68040 turns each C11/C++ atomic into a call into
libatomic, whose AmigaOS build makes it atomic with Forbid() and Permit().

-mcas, off by default, gives a 68020 to 68060 FL_CAS again, so sync.md's
atomic_compare_and_swap patterns are used: CAS inline for 1, 2 and 4 bytes
(the fetch-and-op forms become CAS loops), and 8 bytes stay libatomic
calls.  A program asks for it when its atomics never live in Chip RAM, as
GFOpenBrowser's (heap and data in Fast RAM).  Without -mcas, or on a
68000/68010/CPU32, nothing changes.
---
 gcc/config/m68k/amigaos.opt |  4 ++++
 gcc/config/m68k/m68k.cc     | 11 +++++++++++
 2 files changed, 15 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gcc/config/m68k/amigaos.opt b/gcc/config/m68k/amigaos.opt
--- a/gcc/config/m68k/amigaos.opt
+++ b/gcc/config/m68k/amigaos.opt
@@ -55,3 +55,7 @@
 resident32
 Target Common Var(flag_resident,4)
 data is addressed relative to a4 with 32 bit offsets, linked as resident
+
+mcas
+Target Var(m68k_amiga_cas) Init(0)
+Do 1, 2 and 4 byte atomic operations with CAS on a 68020 to 68060 (data never in Chip RAM)
diff --git a/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.cc b/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.cc
--- a/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.cc
+++ b/gcc/config/m68k/m68k.cc
@@ -628,6 +628,17 @@
 
   m68k_cpu_flags = entry->flags;
 
+#ifdef TARGET_AMIGAOS
+  /* -mcas: CAS for the 1-, 2- and 4-byte atomics on a 68020 to 68060, as
+     GCC does elsewhere.  It is off by default because the chipset does not
+     take CAS's locked read-modify-write cycle in Chip RAM; a program whose
+     atomics never live there asks for it.  */
+  if (m68k_amiga_cas
+      && (m68k_cpu_flags & FL_ISA_68020)
+      && (m68k_cpu_flags & FL_BITFIELD))
+    m68k_cpu_flags |= FL_CAS;
+#endif
+
   /* Use the architecture setting to derive default values for
      certain flags.  */
   target_mask = 0;
-- 
2.53.0

